# The Recommended rule of the same name holds the phrases (`as needed`,
# `where applicable`). These words sit here, because human-written docs
# use them about three times as often as the phrases, and a word rule
# cannot see a criterion named a few words later (`an appropriate label
# matching topologyKey`). Each
# word counts only without `for` or `to` after it, the two words that
# most often introduce the criterion (`suitable for bootstrapping`). The
# fix names the criterion (`choose an appropriate timeout` becomes
# `choose a timeout longer than the slowest request`).
#
# `proper` is left out, because it names a math term (`a proper
# subset`), and `relevant` is left out, because it usually points at a
# named thing (`the relevant API reference`).
#
# It hit 119 lines in the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, and 18 of 20
# sampled hits left the criterion unstated. Both misses named it later
# in the sentence (`an appropriate label matching topologyKey`).
expr word [appropriate, appropriately, properly, suitable, suitably, reasonable, reasonably, sensible, sane]
expr main @word!(( )[for, to])
let message "Vague criterion. Name the condition that makes it right."
let description "Flags `appropriate`, `properly`, `reasonable`, and similar words that leave the reader to supply a criterion."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The image is suitable for bootstrapping the control plane."
allows "The limit is appropriate to a single tenant."
allows "Choose a timeout longer than the slowest request."
